Indeed, he thinks, said world had better end;
He sympathises rather with the fish
Outside, than with the drowned paired beasts within

Exasperated grown beyond control,
From waiting for his soup or fish or mutton.
At last the station’s reached, the engine stops;

fisherman's:
By a babbling brook,
The fisherman's sport we ply;
And list the song

fishes:
Across would drop in terror to the earth;
Fishes would drown; and the all-governing reins
Would tangle in the frantic hands of God

fisher:
Too young to feel afraid;
The fisher folk would give
A kind strange word to me,

fissure:
Her clinging was a thing of dread;
Where'er she touched a fissure spread,
And he who'd breasted many a storm

heed only the shuddering
down in the fissure beneath the rock
of the temple floor;
